WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:03.100
Welcome back.
-I miss you horribly.

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:05.200
When was the last time
I was on this show?

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.434
-It was pre-COVID. I know that.
It's been a while.

00:00:07.434 --> 00:00:08.534
-You did so well.

00:00:08.534 --> 00:00:11.734
Thank you for leading us
through dark times

00:00:11.734 --> 00:00:13.200
and challenging times.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:15.601
-You're a good man.
-You're a light.

00:00:15.601 --> 00:00:17.067
You're a light in the world.

00:00:17.067 --> 00:00:19.767
-You have two little boys.
How old are your boys?

00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:20.968
-Get this.

00:00:20.968 --> 00:00:25.667
The oldest one,
Charlie Ocean, is almost 7.

00:00:25.667 --> 00:00:27.400
He'll be 7 Independence Day.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.501
-Wow! Independence Day.
Isn't that interesting?

00:00:30.501 --> 00:00:31.701
-That's interesting.

00:00:31.701 --> 00:00:34.467
And the younger one is
River Joe.

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:36.467
He just turned 5.

00:00:36.467 --> 00:00:37.934
-That's amazing.
-Isn't that sweet?

00:00:37.934 --> 00:00:41.400
And you have...
-Two little girls -- 8 and 7.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:42.734
So they're around the same --
Yeah.

00:00:42.734 --> 00:00:44.367
-Oh, five years in between.
-Yep.

00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:45.634
-How's that?

00:00:45.634 --> 00:00:49.000
'Cause we have less
than two years between them,

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.033
so they're very --

00:00:50.033 --> 00:00:51.501
-No. I'm sorry. 8 and 7.

00:00:51.501 --> 00:00:55.634
So they're one year in between,
one and a half year.

00:00:55.634 --> 00:00:57.567
Not five year in between, yeah.

00:00:57.567 --> 00:01:01.367
-Would you like to take
a recess and compose yourself

00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:03.167
before we go on with
the cross-examination?

00:01:03.167 --> 00:01:05.400
-Yes, I do.
-3 and 8. You said 3 and 8.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.100
-No, I'm sorry. I meant 8 and 7.
Did I say 3 and 8?

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.334
-Yeah, I believe so.

00:01:09.334 --> 00:01:10.601
Roll the tape back.

00:01:10.601 --> 00:01:13.901
-We could roll the tape back
and see what I said.

00:01:13.901 --> 00:01:15.701
Rick, can we play it back?

00:01:15.701 --> 00:01:17.667
-That's amazing.
-Isn't that sweet?

00:01:17.667 --> 00:01:21.033
And you have...
-Two little girls -- 8 and 7.

00:01:21.033 --> 00:01:22.534
So they're around the same --
Yeah.

00:01:22.534 --> 00:01:23.767
-Oh, five years in between.

00:01:23.767 --> 00:01:25.067
-There was a lot of numbers.

00:01:25.067 --> 00:01:26.300
There was a lot of numbers.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:27.801
I said, "Two little girls --
8 and 7."

00:01:27.801 --> 00:01:31.400
Maybe you subtracted
2 from the 7 and then --

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:33.601
Yeah, that's very possible.

00:01:33.601 --> 00:01:34.901
-I am an idiot. Wow!

00:01:34.901 --> 00:01:36.267
-You would never -- No.

00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:37.534
-I've learned
a great lesson tonight.

00:01:37.534 --> 00:01:39.801
-Now, what was that?
What did you learn? Already?

00:01:39.801 --> 00:01:43.567
-That I'm -- That I have
something wrong with me.

00:01:43.567 --> 00:01:45.534
-That's not true at all.
-The bridges are down.

00:01:45.534 --> 00:01:46.667
The bridges are down.

00:01:46.667 --> 00:01:48.868
-No, they're back up
and they're running.

00:01:48.868 --> 00:01:50.434
-8 and 7.
-8 and 7, yeah.

00:01:50.434 --> 00:01:52.067
-But what are boys like?

00:01:52.067 --> 00:01:54.501
I would have no idea
what to raise --

00:01:54.501 --> 00:01:57.133
-Well, you've seen boys.
You know boys. You've seen boys.

00:01:57.133 --> 00:01:59.267
-They're very energetic.

00:01:59.267 --> 00:02:00.801
-Oh, my God. Yes.

00:02:00.801 --> 00:02:03.567
-I have friends that have boys.
They just jump off of stuff.

00:02:03.567 --> 00:02:04.834
I know. We were talking before.

00:02:04.834 --> 00:02:06.868
I know, "Oh, they're crazy."

00:02:06.868 --> 00:02:10.300
I like to say, first of all,
protect yourself at all times,

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:13.367
because they are really --
They are combative

00:02:13.367 --> 00:02:16.167
and full of -- they want to --
They want to fight.

00:02:16.167 --> 00:02:18.167
I mean, they're taking karate

00:02:18.167 --> 00:02:19.801
and ballet class
and different things.

00:02:19.801 --> 00:02:22.200
But they are like this.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:26.300
And I don't know why I'm
the object of their, you know,

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:28.834
destruction, but they're
like -- And they team up.

00:02:28.834 --> 00:02:30.501
"Get him! Get him!"

00:02:30.501 --> 00:02:33.534
And they want to bring me down.

00:02:33.534 --> 00:02:35.267
-Yeah. Of course.

00:02:35.267 --> 00:02:37.267
Well, you're a big dude.

00:02:37.267 --> 00:02:38.567
-Is that it?
-Yeah, and you like to play.

00:02:38.567 --> 00:02:39.667
You're playful.
They want to play around.

00:02:39.667 --> 00:02:41.067
-Well, I am. I do like to play.

00:02:41.067 --> 00:02:43.968
My wife is kind of bad cop
to my good cop.

00:02:43.968 --> 00:02:46.634
-Yeah. You're too nice to them?
-I'm a little nice.

00:02:46.634 --> 00:02:48.400
I don't like to get --
You know, she says,

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.601
"Hey, don't let them
do that to you.

00:02:50.601 --> 00:02:52.901
And, you know,
make them do this.

00:02:52.901 --> 00:02:54.033
You're the adult. Come on."

00:02:54.033 --> 00:02:55.300
-Yeah, I think my wife
says that, too.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:56.367
But I'm just like,
"Come on, let's go.

00:02:56.367 --> 00:02:58.033
Let's wrestle. Let's do it.
Come on."

00:02:58.033 --> 00:02:59.834
'Cause, yeah, they're going to
get to a certain age where

00:02:59.834 --> 00:03:01.234
it's not fun anymore.

00:03:01.234 --> 00:03:02.901
"Hey, let's go punch dad,"
you know?

00:03:02.901 --> 00:03:05.000
And you go, "You're too old.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:06.267
Too old to be punching dad."

00:03:06.267 --> 00:03:08.968
-You know, I remember the day
when I was like --

00:03:08.968 --> 00:03:11.501
I don't know -- 13 or 14,
when my dad,

00:03:11.501 --> 00:03:15.100
Dr. Harold Goldblum --
I did something wrong.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:19.100
He rolled up a newspaper,
and he went "Bam" and hit me.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:20.934
And I just kind of --
I was, I think,

00:03:20.934 --> 00:03:22.334
taller than he was
at that point.

00:03:22.334 --> 00:03:23.634
-You never forgot it.

00:03:23.634 --> 00:03:26.100
-Well, and I kind of looked at
him, and I could tell --

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.601
I think I remember in that
moment, something changed.

00:03:28.601 --> 00:03:30.033
Something happened.

00:03:30.033 --> 00:03:33.801
-He was like, "Wait a second.
Are you going to hit back?"

00:03:33.801 --> 00:03:35.834
-Although, it happens now
for me already.

00:03:35.834 --> 00:03:38.734
They're faster.
They go "Dada, let's go."

00:03:38.734 --> 00:03:40.367
They outrun me.
They do a lot of things already.

00:03:40.367 --> 00:03:41.634
-Yeah.
-Oh, yeah.

00:03:41.634 --> 00:03:42.834
-Can I just say this,
too, by the way?

00:03:42.834 --> 00:03:44.100
You look fantastic.

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:46.100
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:47.901 --> 00:03:50.133
Beautiful jumpsuit.
-Thank you, Questlove.

00:03:50.133 --> 00:03:51.434
He goes...

00:03:51.434 --> 00:03:52.567
-Yeah, he claps with
his sticks sometimes.

00:03:52.567 --> 00:03:54.234
-By the way,
I so loved that movie.

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:55.534
-"Summer of Soul"?

00:03:55.534 --> 00:04:00.300
-I so loved -- I saw that
way before -- I saw it early on.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:02.000
I adored that. Congratulations.
-He did a good job.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:03.067
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thank you.

00:04:03.067 --> 00:04:05.367
-That was great.
-Loved it.

00:04:05.367 --> 00:04:07.167
Loved it, loved it, loved it.

00:04:07.167 --> 00:04:08.868
-You've actually --

00:04:08.868 --> 00:04:11.334
You've been kind of
a fashion icon your whole life.

00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:14.000
But this is -- No,
but I have to get into this,

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.033
because I was going,
"Jeff, this is fantastic.

00:04:16.033 --> 00:04:17.133
I love this."

00:04:17.133 --> 00:04:19.234
And you're like,
"Yeah, well, no big deal.

00:04:19.234 --> 00:04:21.200
I did a thing with Prada.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:22.701
I was on the runway."

00:04:22.701 --> 00:04:25.067
And I go, "Stop right now.
What did you just say?"

00:04:25.067 --> 00:04:27.334
And you go, "I was a runway
model for Prada."

00:04:27.334 --> 00:04:29.801
I go, "Wait, Jeff.
You can't bury the lead.

00:04:29.801 --> 00:04:31.100
This really happened?"

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:33.334
-Well, I don't know that
I was a runway model.

00:04:33.334 --> 00:04:36.133
I mean, for that day,
I was modeling some clothing

00:04:36.133 --> 00:04:38.234
and I walked on this runway.

00:04:38.234 --> 00:04:40.267
But it was fun. It was fun.

00:04:40.267 --> 00:04:42.300
-We got a clip of it,
by the way.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.200
-What?

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:46.434
-Took us five minutes,
and we already got the clip.

00:04:46.434 --> 00:04:50.167
Here's Jeff Goldblum
on the Prada runway.

00:04:50.167 --> 00:04:51.367
♪♪

00:04:51.367 --> 00:04:52.567
-Uh-oh.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:52.567 --> 00:04:54.300
-Dude, look at you.

00:04:55.934 --> 00:04:57.901
That's great.
-You like that?

00:04:57.901 --> 00:05:01.000
-Yes! Wait. That's what you
look -- You look fantastic.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.133
-Yeah, you never
saw that before?

00:05:02.133 --> 00:05:03.267
-What were you -- No.
-Oh, thank you.

00:05:03.267 --> 00:05:05.334
-We just got it just now.
-Oh, thank you.

00:05:05.334 --> 00:05:07.634
But how do you --
Was that a character

00:05:07.634 --> 00:05:09.100
or is that Jeff Goldblum?

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:11.133
-Well, you know, my imagination
started to -- I don't know.

00:05:11.133 --> 00:05:12.200
It's nothing, really.

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:14.234
But my imagination
did start to work.

00:05:14.234 --> 00:05:15.968
Although, I was trying
mainly not to trip.

00:05:15.968 --> 00:05:17.234
That's what I was
mainly trying to do.

00:05:17.234 --> 00:05:19.701
-That was powerful.
-Well, thank you very much.

00:05:19.701 --> 00:05:21.801
Before, it just occurred to me
they were playing music,

00:05:21.801 --> 00:05:23.100
and I was listening to
the music.

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:25.534
It was unexpected
and unrehearsed.

00:05:25.534 --> 00:05:30.033
But I was thinking of -- Well,
that looks a little kind of --

00:05:30.033 --> 00:05:32.534
I don't think I was doing
kind of something like that.

00:05:32.534 --> 00:05:34.033
But something aggressive.
-It was like...

00:05:34.033 --> 00:05:38.701
♪♪

00:05:38.701 --> 00:05:40.701
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:41.968 --> 00:05:43.234
-It was something like that.
-That was good.

00:05:43.234 --> 00:05:45.267
-Yeah, something like that.
Thank you very much.

00:05:45.267 --> 00:05:46.434
-You got rhythm.

00:05:46.434 --> 00:05:48.701
You know how to do it
and own that stage.

00:05:48.701 --> 00:05:51.234
I would be nervous.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:51.234 --> 00:05:52.868
I was thrilled. I was excited.

00:05:52.868 --> 00:05:55.200
Mrs. Prada,
very, you know, wonderful.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:56.634
Very creative Raf Simons.

00:05:56.634 --> 00:05:59.267
And, so, they gave me this
to wear tonight.

00:05:59.267 --> 00:06:00.634
This is its maiden voyage.

00:06:00.634 --> 00:06:03.868
I've never worn this before
tonight. And these boots.

00:06:03.868 --> 00:06:05.100
[ Cheers and applause ]
Really?

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:07.300
-Prada knows how to do it.
-Well, I don't know.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:09.534
-That's so cool
that you did that.

00:06:09.534 --> 00:06:10.767
-Thank you, thank you.

00:06:10.767 --> 00:06:13.100
-Can we talk about
"Jurassic World: Dominion"?

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:14.367
-Yes, we can.

00:06:14.367 --> 00:06:16.133
Well, you're in the family,
of course,

00:06:16.133 --> 00:06:18.968
because as you all well know,
"Jurassic World,"

00:06:18.968 --> 00:06:21.067
the first one in this trilogy,

00:06:21.067 --> 00:06:23.734
directed by the great
Colin Trevorrow --

00:06:23.734 --> 00:06:25.067
-Colin Trevorrow, by the way --
-Colin Trevorrow.

00:06:25.067 --> 00:06:27.000
-He's a genius.
-He's a genius.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.501
-Genius, yeah.
-Yes. Einstein was a genius.

00:06:29.501 --> 00:06:31.100
Beethoven was a genius.
-Mm-hmm.

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:32.234
-Colin Trevorrow.
-Gandhi.

00:06:32.234 --> 00:06:34.601
Yes, and Colin Trevorrow.
-Gandhi.

00:06:34.601 --> 00:06:36.767
-Yeah.
-You were in the first one.

00:06:36.767 --> 00:06:38.100
-I was in "Jurassic World."

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:40.067
Everyone, please hold your app--
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:40.067 --> 00:06:43.133
Hold your applause, and I'll do
autographs after the show.

00:06:43.133 --> 00:06:45.100
I can't really...
-I'm star-struck.

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:47.267
-How did they make
the other movies without me?

00:06:47.267 --> 00:06:48.567
-I don't know.

00:06:48.567 --> 00:06:50.133
-Yeah, the crowds are
just screaming

00:06:50.133 --> 00:06:51.400
for my character to return.

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:52.868
-I lobbied the whole time.

00:06:52.868 --> 00:06:54.534
I said, "I'll come back if..."

00:06:54.534 --> 00:06:55.834
-"Jimmy's not in it."

00:06:55.834 --> 00:07:00.467
-James Tiberius Fallon --
-Fallon is not in the movie.

00:07:00.467 --> 00:07:02.501
-Yeah. I'm still crestfallen.

00:07:02.501 --> 00:07:05.100
-I've got to say,
it is what we want.

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:07.000
We want a big summer movie.
It's time.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:08.501
We want a big popcorn movie.

00:07:08.501 --> 00:07:10.033
We want -- I don't know how he

00:07:10.033 --> 00:07:11.367
writes and directs
all this stuff.

00:07:11.367 --> 00:07:12.667
-Oh, he's amazing.

00:07:12.667 --> 00:07:15.133
And I think what he's done
with this is fantastic.

00:07:15.133 --> 00:07:18.601
It is more exciting than any of
the other "Jurassic" movies.

00:07:18.601 --> 00:07:22.334
Dinosaurs are all over the world
in this one,

00:07:22.334 --> 00:07:23.968
everywhere we are.

00:07:23.968 --> 00:07:25.133
And it's more thrilling.

00:07:25.133 --> 00:07:26.734
The action never stops.

00:07:26.734 --> 00:07:28.267
I saw it --
I've seen it twice now.

00:07:28.267 --> 00:07:30.634
The last time I saw it,
I was literally --

00:07:30.634 --> 00:07:31.834
This is not -- You know,

00:07:31.834 --> 00:07:33.400
I guess I'm here to say
something like this,

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:35.000
but I'm telling you,
this is true.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:37.434
I was watching it
and I was gripped --

00:07:37.434 --> 00:07:38.634
gripped -- and like this.

00:07:38.634 --> 00:07:39.934
And there are many scares.

00:07:39.934 --> 00:07:43.067
If you like this kind of
thing -- and I do -- I jumped.

00:07:43.067 --> 00:07:45.367
I jumped many, many times.
-Yeah.

00:07:45.367 --> 00:07:47.300
-And I'm very kind of into
the characters.

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:49.868
You know, I'm in it,
the great Laura Dern.

00:07:49.868 --> 00:07:51.133
Oscar winner Laura Dern.
-Oh, yeah, Sam Neill.

00:07:51.133 --> 00:07:52.701
-Sam Neill.
-Laura Dern. I love Laura Dern.

00:07:52.701 --> 00:07:54.534
Who doesn't love Laura Dern?

00:07:54.534 --> 00:07:56.834
-Who does not --
And we're in it kind of a lot.

00:07:56.834 --> 00:07:58.567
-The trilogy -- The team --

00:07:58.567 --> 00:08:00.200
The band got back together
for this.

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:02.133
-The band got back together.

00:08:02.133 --> 00:08:03.567
We would be --
What band would we be?

00:08:03.567 --> 00:08:04.701
Everybody says "band."

00:08:04.701 --> 00:08:06.667
Would we be
Peter, Paul and Mary?

00:08:06.667 --> 00:08:08.067
Yes, of course. That's right.
-That's fantastic, yeah.

00:08:08.067 --> 00:08:09.434
-That's right.
-Yeah.

00:08:09.434 --> 00:08:12.200
That's exactly who you'd be.
-Or Tony Orlando and Dawn?

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:13.934
-And Dawn? Yeah.
I'm trying to think --

00:08:13.934 --> 00:08:17.968
-♪ Tie a yellow ribbon
around the old oak tree ♪

00:08:17.968 --> 00:08:20.234
-♪ It's been three long years ♪

00:08:20.234 --> 00:08:21.601
-Very good.

00:08:21.601 --> 00:08:23.901
So, yeah, we're back together
and we're mashed up with

00:08:23.901 --> 00:08:27.167
the great Chris Pratt,
whom you know and adore.

00:08:27.167 --> 00:08:28.701
-Bryce Dallas Howard.

00:08:28.701 --> 00:08:33.534
-And Bryce Dallas Howard,
DeWanda Wise, Mamoudou Athie.

00:08:33.534 --> 00:08:35.033
-I have a photo of you
from the movie.

00:08:35.033 --> 00:08:36.267
Here's a picture of Jeff
from the film.

00:08:36.267 --> 00:08:37.601
-Yes.
-Here you go.

00:08:37.601 --> 00:08:40.434
-Yes. They did --
-Now, wait.

00:08:40.434 --> 00:08:41.634
-They did that thing
that they did

00:08:41.634 --> 00:08:43.634
to De Niro in "The Irishman."

00:08:43.634 --> 00:08:46.033
They made me look much younger.

00:08:46.033 --> 00:08:49.100
-No, I love this. Thank you.
We actually found this photo.

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:50.734
I think it was from the
last time you were on the show,

00:08:50.734 --> 00:08:51.834
because we were going to
talk about this.

00:08:51.834 --> 00:08:53.667
But look how stylish you
were back then.

00:08:53.667 --> 00:08:55.501
Prada -- Look out, Prada.

00:08:55.501 --> 00:08:59.534
-Well, I apologize for
the Speedo.

00:08:59.534 --> 00:09:02.200
-Don't apologize for anything.
We're thanking you for this.

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:04.300
-And it's a little bit like that
pose from the first run.

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:06.534
But that's my backyard
in Pittsburgh.

00:09:06.534 --> 00:09:07.934
I grew up in Pittsburgh,
West Homestead,

00:09:07.934 --> 00:09:09.801
and that's our backyard.

00:09:09.801 --> 00:09:11.067
I'll be darned.
-Yeah. Right?

00:09:11.067 --> 00:09:12.868
And you knew how to work it
back then, as well.

00:09:12.868 --> 00:09:14.334
Yeah, see?
Prada knows something.

00:09:14.334 --> 00:09:16.367
-The glasses are similar,
not dissimilar.

00:09:16.367 --> 00:09:20.033
-But this reminded me of this
scene from "Jurassic..."

00:09:20.033 --> 00:09:23.334
[ Cheers and applause ]
-That's what I said.

00:09:23.334 --> 00:09:25.334
[ Laughter ]
♪♪

00:09:29.467 --> 00:09:33.968
-But you know what's cool is
that they're making --

00:09:33.968 --> 00:09:36.200
-Something's wrong with me.
-No, no, no, no.

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:37.934
They're making merch
for this movie

00:09:37.934 --> 00:09:41.267
because people are just -- It's
the biggest thing in the world.

00:09:41.267 --> 00:09:43.200
It was the biggest movie
in the world.

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:45.033
And they made this toy.

00:09:45.033 --> 00:09:46.701
Well, I wouldn't say it's --
Well, it's a toy.

00:09:46.701 --> 00:09:48.400
It's an action figure.
-Yes, yes.

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:50.100
-Yep. This is your great book.

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:51.234
-Yeah. Yeah.
-Yep.

00:09:51.234 --> 00:09:52.434
-This is your book that your --

00:09:52.434 --> 00:09:55.200
-They mocked that up,
yeah, as if I -- as if --

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:56.834
Dr. Ian Malcolm -- that's
the name of my character --

00:09:56.834 --> 00:09:59.400
wrote "God Creates Dinosaurs."

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:01.968
-And then, what you get
when you get this...

00:10:02.801 --> 00:10:04.801
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:10:10.567 --> 00:10:13.601
-Boink!
[ Both laugh ]

00:10:13.601 --> 00:10:16.467
-Oh, very good.
-Yeah. You're a stud there.

00:10:16.467 --> 00:10:18.534
Come on, bud.
-Oh, yes. Yeah.

00:10:18.534 --> 00:10:20.067
-Did Spielberg ask you to go --

00:10:20.067 --> 00:10:22.067
"Hey, Jeff,
just open up another button."

00:10:22.067 --> 00:10:23.200
-No, no.

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:24.701
I don't remember how
that happened, actually.

00:10:24.701 --> 00:10:25.801
It was 30 years ago.

00:10:25.801 --> 00:10:28.434
And, no, here's what --
Laura Dern --

00:10:28.434 --> 00:10:30.601
I was talking to her yesterday.

00:10:30.601 --> 00:10:32.667
And she -- Because I said --
People have asked me that.

00:10:32.667 --> 00:10:34.100
"How did it happen?"

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:36.968
Did I say, "Steven, please,
let me -- As long as I'm here,

00:10:36.968 --> 00:10:38.334
you know,
you don't want to miss this,"

00:10:38.334 --> 00:10:40.000
or did he say, "Jeff, please,

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:42.467
come on, let's see something"?

00:10:42.467 --> 00:10:44.267
No. I don't think
those things happened.

00:10:44.267 --> 00:10:48.133
I think in the,
you know, actorly way,

00:10:48.133 --> 00:10:49.367
I think my character --

00:10:49.367 --> 00:10:51.467
Even though you see
this thing on the leg --

00:10:51.467 --> 00:10:53.834
My leg, of course,
had some difficulty.

00:10:53.834 --> 00:10:57.300
I think I had an injury
that is not depicted here.

00:10:57.300 --> 00:10:59.367
But in the movie, whether you
see it below the screen or not,

00:10:59.367 --> 00:11:00.934
there's something
going on there.

00:11:00.934 --> 00:11:03.234
If you can get some evidence
for that, you know,

00:11:03.234 --> 00:11:04.667
that will be my story.

00:11:04.667 --> 00:11:05.901
-No, that's not --
-But wait a minute.

00:11:05.901 --> 00:11:07.667
Wait a minute.
I see some blood there.

00:11:07.667 --> 00:11:09.000
There's a blood spatter there.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:12.067
-That? That is the reason
you had to remove your shirt?

00:11:12.067 --> 00:11:13.167
-Yes.
-Because of that?

00:11:13.167 --> 00:11:15.667
-Well...
-That is a nick.

00:11:15.667 --> 00:11:17.067
That is barely anything.

00:11:17.067 --> 00:11:18.100
You don't need to take
your shirt off for that.

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:19.367
-That's excess.

00:11:19.367 --> 00:11:21.133
That's excess from something
that was happening here.

00:11:21.133 --> 00:11:22.434
I'm telling you.

00:11:22.434 --> 00:11:25.167
-Either way, it became a scene
that we all remember

00:11:25.167 --> 00:11:26.634
from the movie,
and you're in a movie with

00:11:26.634 --> 00:11:29.334
CGI, giant dinosaurs, and, yet,

00:11:29.334 --> 00:11:31.501
this is what
we want to play with.

00:11:31.501 --> 00:11:32.567
This is --
-I know.

00:11:32.567 --> 00:11:34.267
-Yeah.
-Who can blame us?

00:11:34.267 --> 00:11:36.767
-What can we say about this --

00:11:36.767 --> 00:11:39.033
This is the last
"Jurassic World."

00:11:39.033 --> 00:11:40.801
-Oh, maybe, maybe.

00:11:40.801 --> 00:11:42.968
I mean, it does,
sort of in a delicious way,

00:11:42.968 --> 00:11:46.100
kind of bring us to a sense of
closure and wholeness

00:11:46.100 --> 00:11:47.667
from these six movies,

00:11:47.667 --> 00:11:49.801
you know, the three parks
and the three worlds.

00:11:49.801 --> 00:11:52.300
and all the characters
kind of find some

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:57.067
beautiful...comeuppance
and all that.

00:11:57.067 --> 00:11:59.834
But, you know,
this is a chapter closer,

00:11:59.834 --> 00:12:02.000
but there may be
more things to the book.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:03.300
I don't know.
-Oh, really?

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:04.834
-Well, I don't know.

00:12:04.834 --> 00:12:07.267
If they like it -- You know, we
offer this humbly at your feet,

00:12:07.267 --> 00:12:09.834
and if you want to see it and
you find some pleasure in it --

00:12:09.834 --> 00:12:13.434
-Yes! Are you kidding me?
We cannot wait for this movie.

00:12:13.434 --> 00:12:15.801
[ Cheers and applause ]
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:12:15.801 --> 00:12:17.133
You're fantastic.

00:12:17.133 --> 00:12:19.868
Here's Jeff Goldblum in
"Jurassic World: Dominion."

00:12:19.868 --> 00:12:21.267
Take a look at this.
-Yeah, grazie.

00:12:21.267 --> 00:12:24.501
-We're talking about cascading
system-wide effects, Ian.

00:12:24.501 --> 00:12:26.133
-Gosh, that's a drag.

00:12:26.133 --> 00:12:28.567
-What is the matter with you?

00:12:28.567 --> 00:12:30.801
-Why? Is there something special
you wanted me to do?

00:12:30.801 --> 00:12:32.267
-I mean, you know,
if it's not too much to care.

00:12:32.267 --> 00:12:36.334
-Hey, I-I gave my opinion
robustly for years.

00:12:36.334 --> 00:12:38.367
As expected,
the sum of our human endeavors

00:12:38.367 --> 00:12:40.100
has led to our annihilation.

00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:44.801
And the only play now is to take
the time we have left and, uh,

00:12:44.801 --> 00:12:47.100
you know, just like
we always do, squander it.

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:48.868
-Yeah, that's what
I'm talking about.

00:12:48.868 --> 00:12:50.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:12:50.534 --> 00:12:51.901
Jeff Goldblum right there.

00:12:51.901 --> 00:12:53.934
Looking good, by the way.

00:12:53.934 --> 00:12:55.534
"Jurassic World: Dominion"

00:12:55.534 --> 00:12:58.067
opens in theaters and IMAX
this Friday.

00:12:58.067 --> 00:12:59.133
Get your tickets now.

